  "context": [
    "The Venue arrived in 2019 as Hyundai's answer to the then-emerging compact-SUV boom in India, and the 2023 facelift carried it into its current form with a revised face, bigger touchscreen, and a dedicated N Line sporty variant. It remains one of the best-selling nameplates in its segment, and that volume keeps parts and service readily available at Hyundai's wide dealer network, which supports resale confidence outside metros.",
    "Hyundai dropped the 1.5-litre diesel option from the Venue lineup as the segment shifted decisively toward petrol, leaving two petrol engines today: a naturally aspirated 1.2-litre for value-focused buyers and a 1.0-litre turbo-petrol, available with a 6-speed manual, iMT clutchless manual, or 7-speed DCT, for buyers who want more performance. In the used market, turbo-DCT Venues hold a noticeable premium over the 1.2 manual at the same age, reflecting how strongly feature-rich variants are sought after in this segment.",
    "Compact SUVs as a class have displaced hatchbacks as the default first car for a large share of urban Indian buyers over the last five years, and the Venue has been a consistent beneficiary of that shift. Resale demand is strongest in cities with younger, SUV-preferring buyer pools, while more price-sensitive markets continue to favour the cheaper 1.2-litre variants.",
    "The N Line variant, introduced as a sportier trim rather than a mechanically distinct model, trades close to the equivalent SX(O) turbo-DCT variant at resale. Buyers value the cosmetic differentiation but are not yet paying a structural premium for it the way they might for a genuinely different model."
  ],
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is the resale value of a Hyundai Venue?",
      "a": "A well-maintained Venue retains about 72% of its ex-showroom price after two years and roughly 50% after five years, based on transaction data across Indian metros as of September 2026. A 2022 SX 1.0 turbo-petrol with 25,000-50,000 km typically trades around ₹7.0-8.0 lakh depending on city and transmission."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does the turbo-DCT Venue hold value better than the 1.2 manual?",
      "a": "Yes. Turbo-DCT variants consistently trade at a premium over the 1.2-litre manual at equivalent age and mileage, because feature-rich, automatic compact SUVs see stronger used-buyer demand in metros. The premium is most visible in Mumbai and Bengaluru, both running a positive city variance in this data."
    },
    {
      "q": "How does the Venue compare with the Kia Sonet or Nissan Magnite for resale?",
      "a": "The Venue's five-year retention of roughly 50% is a shade above the compact-SUV segment average of about 48% used here, benefiting from Hyundai's dealer density and strong brand trust relative to newer entrants in the segment."
    },
    {
      "q": "How much does a Venue depreciate per year?",
      "a": "The Venue depreciates around 17% in year one, a further 9 points in year two, and then 6-7% annually through year five, landing at roughly 50% retention."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which city gives the best resale price for a Venue?",
      "a": "Bengaluru and Delhi NCR currently trade at about +3% above the national mid, driven by strong SUV demand among younger buyers, while Kolkata trades roughly -2% below it."
    }
  ],
